Chapter: PixelBuf

 A flat XRGB pixel buffer that allocates nothing per pixel. One
 `alloc-bytes` block at construction, `poke-32` and `peek-32` to write and
 read, O(1) per pixel, and no heap traffic afterwards.

 This is the framebuffer a bare-metal GUI can actually use, and the
 measurement that says so is worth keeping because the alternative looks
 reasonable. `Game chapter Rasterizer`'s `Framebuf` holds its pixels as a
 `List Integer` and `fb-set` returns a `Framebuf`, so every pixel write
 builds a fresh record. Measured 2026-07-28 against seed B9875136, by
 reading the bump allocator through `alloc-bytes 0` either side of a
 4096-pixel loop:

 | | per pixel | one 1024x768 pass |
 |---|---:|---:|
 | `fb-set` | 24 bytes | 18.9 MB |
 | `poke-32` here | 0 bytes | 0 |

 A control loop that allocates nothing reads 0, so those figures are the
 abstraction and not the instrument. There is no collector, so 18.9 MB per
 full-screen pass is permanent and a repainting shell exhausts the heap in
 a handful of frames. `Framebuf` is right for an offline rasterizer, which
 is where it lives; it cannot back a screen.

 These definitions were in `apps/guios/GuiDisplay.codex` and are moved here
 unchanged so that any application can reach them, rather than the one
 that happened to need them first. **The names are historical.** `gop-`
 names the UEFI Graphics Output Protocol, which is where the buffer is
 usually presented, but nothing in this chapter knows about GOP or about
 any hardware: it is a flat buffer at an address. Renaming is a separate
 mechanical change across roughly 150 call sites and is deliberately not
 bundled here.

 We say:

Section: Flat Pixel Buffer

  GopBuf = record {
    gb-base : Integer,
    gb-width : Integer,
    gb-height : Integer
  }

  gop-buf-new : Integer, Integer, Integer -> GopBuf
  gop-buf-new (w) (h) (bg) =
    let size = w * h * 4
    in let base = alloc-bytes size
    in let dummy = gop-buf-fill-loop base bg 0 (w * h)
    in GopBuf { gb-base = base, gb-width = w, gb-height = h }

 `alloc-bytes` is a bare bump and does not zero, so the fill is what makes
 a new buffer defined rather than whatever the heap last held there.

  gop-buf-fill-loop : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-fill-loop (base) (color) (i) (total) =
    if i >= total then 0
    else let dummy = poke-32 base (i * 4) color
    in gop-buf-fill-loop base color (i + 1) total

  gop-buf-set : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-set (gb) (x) (y) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= gb.gb-width then 0
    else if y >= gb.gb-height then 0
    else poke-32 (gb.gb-base) ((y * gb.gb-width + x) * 4) color

  gop-buf-get : GopBuf, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-get (gb) (x) (y) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= gb.gb-width then 0
    else if y >= gb.gb-height then 0
    else peek-32 (gb.gb-base) ((y * gb.gb-width + x) * 4)

  gop-buf-clear : GopBuf, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-clear (gb) (color) =
    gop-buf-fill-loop (gb.gb-base) color 0 (gb.gb-width * gb.gb-height)

  gop-buf-rect : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-rect (gb) (x) (y) (w) (h) (color) =
    gop-buf-rect-rows gb x y w h color 0

  gop-buf-rect-rows : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-rect-rows (gb) (x) (y) (w) (h) (color) (row) =
    if row >= h then 0
    else let dummy = gop-buf-hline gb x (x + w - 1) (y + row) color
    in gop-buf-rect-rows gb x y w h color (row + 1)

  gop-buf-hline : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-hline (gb) (x0) (x1) (y) (color) =
    if y < 0 then 0
    else if y >= gb.gb-height then 0
    else let cx0 = if x0 < 0 then 0 else x0
    in let cx1 = if x1 >= gb.gb-width then gb.gb-width - 1 else x1
    in gop-buf-hline-loop gb cx0 cx1 y color

  gop-buf-hline-loop : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-hline-loop (gb) (x) (x1) (y) (color) =
    if x > x1 then 0
    else let dummy = poke-32 (gb.gb-base) ((y * gb.gb-width + x) * 4) color
    in gop-buf-hline-loop gb (x + 1) x1 y color

Section: Outlined Rect

  gop-buf-outline : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-outline (gb) (x) (y) (w) (h) (color) =
    let dummy = gop-buf-hline gb x (x + w - 1) y color
    in let dummy2 = gop-buf-hline gb x (x + w - 1) (y + h - 1) color
    in let dummy3 = gop-buf-vline gb x y (y + h - 1) color
    in gop-buf-vline gb (x + w - 1) y (y + h - 1) color

  gop-buf-vline : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-vline (gb) (x) (y0) (y1) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if x >= gb.gb-width then 0
    else gop-buf-vline-loop gb x y0 y1 color

  gop-buf-vline-loop : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gop-buf-vline-loop (gb) (x) (y) (y1) (color) =
    if y > y1 then 0
    else if y < 0 then gop-buf-vline-loop gb x (y + 1) y1 color
    else if y >= gb.gb-height then 0
    else let dummy = poke-32 (gb.gb-base) ((y * gb.gb-width + x) * 4) color
    in gop-buf-vline-loop gb x (y + 1) y1 color

Section: Alpha Blend

 Alpha is 0 to 255, the scale the byte in a glyph coverage map already
 carries, so a caller never has to rescale one.

 There is a second blend in this quire, `comp-blend-pixel` in
 `UI chapter Surface`, and it takes alpha 0 to 1000. Neither is called
 from outside its own chapter today, so nothing is currently wrong; the
 hazard is that they are one operation under two scales, and a 255-scale
 alpha handed to the 1000-scale one is a quarter opacity rather than
 opaque, which looks like a rendering choice instead of a bug. They cannot
 be merged while they sit on different buffer types, which is the reason
 this chapter exists.

 The shortcut at 240 is not an approximation of the arithmetic: it skips
 the read-modify-write entirely for a coverage byte that is opaque within
 one part in sixteen, which is the common case for the interior of a
 glyph.

  gbf-blend-pixel : GopBuf, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  gbf-blend-pixel (gb) (x) (y) (fg) (alpha) =
    if alpha >= 240 then gop-buf-set gb x y fg
    else let bg = gop-buf-get gb x y
    in let fr = bit-and (bit-shru fg 16) 255
    in let fg2 = bit-and (bit-shru fg 8) 255
    in let fb = bit-and fg 255
    in let br = bit-and (bit-shru bg 16) 255
    in let bg2 = bit-and (bit-shru bg 8) 255
    in let bb = bit-and bg 255
    in let rr = br + (fr - br) * alpha / 255
    in let rg = bg2 + (fg2 - bg2) * alpha / 255
    in let rb = bb + (fb - bb) * alpha / 255
    in gop-buf-set gb x y (bit-shl rr 16 + bit-shl rg 8 + rb)
